WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:04.559
Welcome to Module Three: Control Plane Installation and Configuration.

2
00:00:04.889 --> 00:00:09.810
It's about the software that turns a room of GPU servers into one managed cluster.

3
00:00:10.610 --> 00:00:15.595
Picture a research lab that set up sixty-four GPU nodes by hand, one at a time.

4
00:00:15.925 --> 00:00:19.672
Every node was installed a little differently, and nobody wrote down how.

5
00:00:20.472 --> 00:00:24.198
Within months, driver versions had drifted apart across the cluster.

6
00:00:24.528 --> 00:00:27.291
Jobs that ran on one node crashed on the next,

7
00:00:27.341 --> 00:00:29.982
and Fabric Manager refused to start on some.

8
00:00:30.782 --> 00:00:33.676
Then, one Friday evening, the single head node died.

9
00:00:34.006 --> 00:00:36.901
Scheduling, provisioning, and logins went with it,

10
00:00:36.951 --> 00:00:39.375
so the whole cluster sat idle all weekend.

11
00:00:40.175 --> 00:00:43.773
Meanwhile, users couldn't run containers on the GPUs at all.

12
00:00:44.103 --> 00:00:48.639
Every researcher built a private software stack, and no result was reproducible.

13
00:00:49.439 --> 00:00:54.317
So the team rebuilt the control plane with NVIDIA Base Command Manager, or BCM.

14
00:00:54.647 --> 00:00:59.333
One software image per node category now provisions every node identically.

15
00:01:00.133 --> 00:01:02.921
They added a second head node, forming an HA pair.

16
00:01:03.251 --> 00:01:07.595
If the active head fails, the passive one takes over, and the cluster keeps running.

17
00:01:08.395 --> 00:01:12.505
Slurm now schedules the work, with Enroot and Pyxis running containers.

18
00:01:12.835 --> 00:01:18.396
Researchers pull tested images from NGC and launch them with a single srun command.

19
00:01:19.196 --> 00:01:22.346
Lesson one installs BCM on the first head node.

20
00:01:22.676 --> 00:01:27.533
Then you add a second head and configure HA failover with a shared IP address.

21
00:01:28.333 --> 00:01:32.955
Lesson two covers OS installation, software images, and node categories.

22
00:01:33.285 --> 00:01:37.843
You'll configure network interfaces, then deploy Slurm with Enroot and Pyxis.

23
00:01:38.643 --> 00:01:43.051
Lesson three installs, updates, and removes GPU and DOCA drivers.

24
00:01:43.381 --> 00:01:47.897
You'll see why Fabric Manager must match the GPU driver version exactly.

25
00:01:48.697 --> 00:01:53.617
Lesson four sets up the NVIDIA Container Toolkit, so Docker can see the GPUs.

26
00:01:53.947 --> 00:01:58.015
One flag, gpus all, hands every GPU to a container.

27
00:01:58.815 --> 00:02:04.589
You'll also use the NGC CLI to browse the catalog and pull containers and models.

28
00:02:04.919 --> 00:02:07.727
An API key links it to your organization and team.

29
00:02:08.527 --> 00:02:11.656
By the end, you could rebuild that lab's cluster the right way.

30
00:02:11.986 --> 00:02:16.821
Identical nodes, a head node that survives failure, and containers that just work.

31
00:02:17.621 --> 00:02:20.170
This domain is nineteen percent of the exam,

32
00:02:20.220 --> 00:02:23.182
with questions on commands, versions, and failover.

33
00:02:23.512 --> 00:02:25.467
Lesson one starts at the head node.
